I didn't migrate anything. All three file writes were denied permission, so nothing in the repo has changed and I didn't run the tests.

Here is what I was going to write. It's ready to apply once you allow writes:

**`src/services/user-service.js`**: drop the `query`/`update` imports and call `UserRepository` instead. Each function now returns a promise.
```js
import { UserRepository } from '../repositories/user-repository.js';

const users = new UserRepository();

export function listActiveUsers() {
  return users.findActive();
}

export function findUser(id) {
  return users.findById(id);
}

export function softDeleteUser(id, when) {
  return users.softDelete(id, when);
}
```

**`tests/user-service.test.js`**: change the two tests from callback-and-`done` style to `async`/`await`. The assertions stay the same, except the `err === null` checks go away.

**`src/api/routes.js`**: this is outside what the migration covers, but it needs changing. The `/users` and `/users/:id` routes pass a callback into these functions. After the migration that callback would never be called, so both endpoints would hang. The fix connects each promise back to the callback:
```js
handler: (req, cb) => listActiveUsers().then((r) => cb(null, r), cb)
handler: (req, cb) => findUser(Number(req.params.id)).then((r) => cb(null, r), cb)
```

Once writes are allowed, I'll apply these and run `node --test tests/user-service.test.js`. Tell me if you'd rather I leave `routes.js` alone.

